🐛 Describe the bug
There seems to be a bug when running a custom op that behaves like a tensor constructor and doesn't have a fake impl registered --

I have a custom op that does not take in any tensors and returns a singular tensor
If I don't register a fake impl for this function, we will go into this check, and the custom op will return true for behaving like a tensor constructor.
We will then call this constructor function, which will automatically pass the function a "device" kwarg. This will cause an error when tracing, where my custom op expects 1 input but is given 2.

    def test_custom_op(self):
        lib = torch.library.Library("mylib", "FRAGMENT")
        lib.define("moo(int x) -> Tensor")

        @torch.library.impl("mylib::moo", "CompositeExplicitAutograd", lib=lib)
        def moo(x):
            return torch.empty(3).fill_(x)

        def f(x):
            b = x.item()
            return torch.ops.mylib.moo(b)

        print(f(torch.tensor(4)))

        gm = make_fx(f, tracing_mode="symbolic")(torch.tensor(4))
        print(gm)
